STEP 12: VERIFY LAMBDA #2 WORKED

Go to:

CloudWatch → Log groups → /aws/lambda/register-model-version-lambda

You want to see:
- no exceptions
- a returned newModelPackageArn
- the productionArtifactUri

Then switch to the Control Plane account and go to:

SageMaker → Model registry → your model package group

Open your model package group, for example:

credit-risk-model

You should see:
- a new version added
- that version’s model data points to the Production S3 artifact
- its approval status set to Approved if that is what you passed
- older approved versions switched to Rejected by the Lambda, if any existed

Creating a new version inside the model group is the expected SageMaker registry behavior, and approval changes are done by updating the model package version rather than the whole group.

---

WHAT VALUES YOU NEED TO REPLACE BEFORE TESTING

There are a few placeholders you must replace in the event payload:

Replace these with real values:

- credit-risk-model → your real Control Plane model package group name
- 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-train:latest → your real training image
- 123456789012.dkr.ecr.us-east-1.amazonaws.com/my-infer:latest → your real inference image
- arn:aws:iam::999999999999:role/SageMakerTrainingExecutionRole → your real SageMaker training role in Production
- s3://prod-ml-data/credit-risk/train/ → your real training input path
- s3://prod-ml-artifacts/credit-risk/retrain/ → your real output path
- arn:aws:sagemaker:us-east-1:111111111111:model-package/credit-risk-model/5 → the original or baseline model package ARN you want to link back to
- CONTROL_PLANE_ROLE_ARN environment variable → your real Control Plane registration role

---

EASIEST WAY TO TEST WITHOUT REAL TRAINING LOGIC

If your real training container is not ready yet, you have two easy choices:

1. use an already working training image you know can finish successfully
2. temporarily swap the training flow to a very simple built-in or known-good container just to prove the event chain works

That way you’re testing the orchestration first:
- EventBridge
- Lambda
- training completion event
- model version registration

and not getting stuck on model code bugs.

---

WHAT “DEPRECATING” OLD VERSIONS MEANS HERE

In your current code, “deprecating” is implemented as:

- newest version gets Approved
- all older Approved versions get changed to Rejected

That does not delete the old versions. It just makes your newest one the only approved candidate, which is the normal governance pattern in SageMaker Model Registry.

---

THE ONE ARCHITECTURAL CAVEAT

Since your new Control Plane model version points to Production S3, any downstream deployment that uses that version must be able to read the Production artifact.

For this exact tutorial, though, both EventBridge rules are in Production, and only the model registration crosses accounts through sts:AssumeRole, which is the simpler way to start.

---

IF SOMETHING FAILS, CHECK IN THIS ORDER

1. Did the custom event get sent?
   Check the output of aws events put-events.

2. Did Rule #1 fire?
   Check start-retraining-lambda CloudWatch logs.

3. Was the SageMaker training job created?
   Check SageMaker Training Jobs page.

4. Did the training job complete?
   If not, fix the training image/input/output issue first.

5. Did Rule #2 fire?
   Check register-model-version-lambda CloudWatch logs.

6. Did Lambda #2 assume the Control Plane role and create the model package version?
   If not, the failing step will usually show clearly in the logs.

---

SIMPLEST MENTAL MODEL

Think of it like this:

- Event 1 says: “start retraining”
- Lambda 1 starts SageMaker retraining
- SageMaker says: “training is done”
- Lambda 2 says: “great, now register this as the new version in Control Plane”

That is the whole loop.

